A||||4||GREENDANO||'88 suspension?
Z||000000||GREENDANO||06-22-99||08:15 PM||||I have access to a full '88 front suspension from a friend of a friend.  Is this something anyone is interested in?  It also has the rear subframe in it too.  Are the V6 subframes different than the fours?  FYI, the car in question has a four cylinder.  What about swaps?  Is there a posibility of a front suspensions swap into a pre-88, or is this a complete car redesign problem which would be more trouble than it's worth?<br>  Dan-O
Z||000001||batboy||06-22-99||08:30 PM||||I'm curious to know if the front suspension assembly is interchangable too. If so, that would be a great upgrade for an older Fiero. YellowFormula just got an 88 too. Maybe between the two of you, this question will get an answer (I hope, hint, hint).
Z||000002||PaUL||06-22-99||08:42 PM||||I believe the attaching hardpoints for both the 88 and older front subframes are the same.
Z||000003||theogre||06-22-99||10:17 PM||||I got really friendly with my suspension lately,,,,, changing all the wheel bearings on a 133K+ mile car does that... ((BTW, There are some things to look out for, really annoying things, when changing the rear hub units, and front wheel bearings. I'll post yet anohter "paper" about it soon.))<p>Mine is an 87. There are allot of anchor points in that thing. Previously I had thought it to be a drop in assembled thing, it's not.<p>It looks like there is a long cross member, but the upper A arm is not attached to it. That Whole top end it connected to the frame as a seperate item. DANGER: if you are removing this assembly then you must remove the springs first! If you don't you will never get it together in another car, assuming you don't kill yourself getting it out.<p>Assuming that the 88 frame is the same as the rest, it should be posible to move both the front and rear suspensions to the pre 88's. If the 88's follow the same design as the rest, there should be No diferance in cradles for the 4 or 6 cyl setups.<p>Alot of this is a big IF. I know from many years of dealing with cars and other things that assembly line makers of things heartily resist large changes that might cause changes to the assembly system. Small things, like replacement of the TBI, or cam, on the 4 cyl are easy. changing the cradle is fairly easy. Those are just changes of parts in a bin. Things like changing the line the car is made on is next to imposible. Changes to large items like frame units are expensive and therefore also dificult. But then GM has repeatedly shown it has no sense at all so anything is posible and all bets are off. 
Z||000004||mwbackus||06-23-99||04:40 AM||||The 88 cradle can be installed in earlier models.  Ryane Motorsports makes an adapter for this application :<p>http://www.ryanemotorsports.com/88cradle.htm<p>The front suspension is a different story.  I recently read an article by someone who was considering it.  I believe he actually removed the crossmembers to compare and determine what was involved.  It does not simply bolt on, and he concluded that the cutting and welding needed would not be pratical.  So the answer to your question IMO :  rear yes, front no.
